      "question": "Design an in-memory Rate Limiter / LRU Cache for caching test authentication tokens. Explain how HashMap + Doubly Linked List achieves O(1) get and O(1) put operations.",
      "expectedFocus": "HashMap maps key to Node. Doubly Linked List maintains access order (head = most recent, tail = least recent). Node contains prev, next, key, value. Evicting from tail and moving accessed node to head in O(1).",
      "answerSummary": "### 1. Conceptual Breakdown (Plain English)\n**What it is:** Combine a Map<string, Node> for O(1) lookups with a Doubly Linked List (pseudo head and tail) for O(1) insertion and removal.\n**Why it matters in QA:** Understanding this concept enables QA engineers to bridge the gap between business specifications and technical verification, eliminating guesswork and test flakiness.\n\n### 2. Deep-Dive Technical Explanation\nCombine a Map<string, Node> for O(1) lookups with a Doubly Linked List (pseudo head and tail) for O(1) insertion and removal. On get(key): lookup node in map and move it to head. On put(key, val): if key exists, update value and move to head; if new, create node and insert at head. If capacity exceeded, remove tail.prev from list and delete its key from map.\n\n### 3. Step-by-Step Practical Application Guide\n• **Step 1: Identify Preconditions & Scope:** Clarify the exact system boundary, inputs, and expected environmental states before executing.\n• **Step 2: Construct Deterministic Assertions:** Avoid relying on arbitrary delays; instead, assert against explicit system state transitions and verifiable outcomes.\n• **Step 3: Isolate Environmental Variables:** Ensure test data is cleanly provisioned and cleaned up so parallel or subsequent runs remain independent.\n\n### 4. Key Terminology for QA Engineers\n• **Deterministic Testing:** A testing approach where a given input always produces the exact same observable outcome without intermittent or random failures.\n• **Flakiness (Flaky Test):** A test that intermittently passes and fails on identical application builds without any code changes, usually caused by timing race conditions or shared mutable state.\n• **Quality Gate:** An automated checkpoint in the CI/CD pipeline that blocks flawed builds from progressing to staging or production.",
      "codeSnippet": "class LRUCache<K, V> {\n  private capacity: number;\n  private map = new Map<K, Node<K, V>>();\n  private head = new Node<K, V>(null as any, null as any);\n  private tail = new Node<K, V>(null as any, null as any);\n\n  constructor(cap: number) {\n    this.capacity = cap;\n    this.head.next = this.tail;\n    this.tail.prev = this.head;\n  }\n  // O(1) get & put implementation\n}",
      "bestPractice": "Use sentinel dummy head and tail nodes to avoid null pointer checks when inserting or deleting elements.",
