      "topic": "Sliding Window: Longest Substring Without Repeating Characters",
      "question": "In automation log analysis, you need to find the length of the longest contiguous sub-sequence of unique log event tokens. Solve the Longest Substring Without Repeating Characters problem with optimal time and space complexity.",
      "expectedFocus": "Sliding window technique with two pointers (left, right), Set or Map storing character -> last seen index, O(N) time complexity, O(min(N, M)) space complexity.",
      "answerSummary": "### 1. Conceptual Breakdown (Plain English)\n**What it is:** Use a sliding window with two pointers (left and right) and a HashMap mapping character -> last seen index.\n**Why it matters in QA:** Understanding this concept enables QA engineers to bridge the gap between business specifications and technical verification, eliminating guesswork and test flakiness.\n\n### 2. Deep-Dive Technical Explanation\nUse a sliding window with two pointers (left and right) and a HashMap mapping character -> last seen index. As right pointer expands across the string, if s[right] exists in the map and its index >= left, jump left to map.get(s[right]) + 1. Update max length as right - left + 1, and update map. Time: O(N) single pass, Space: O(min(N, alphabet size)).\n\n### 3. Step-by-Step Practical Application Guide\n• **Step 1: Identify Preconditions & Scope:** Clarify the exact system boundary, inputs, and expected environmental states before executing.\n• **Step 2: Construct Deterministic Assertions:** Avoid relying on arbitrary delays; instead, assert against explicit system state transitions and verifiable outcomes.\n• **Step 3: Isolate Environmental Variables:** Ensure test data is cleanly provisioned and cleaned up so parallel or subsequent runs remain independent.\n\n### 4. Key Terminology for QA Engineers\n• **Deterministic Testing:** A testing approach where a given input always produces the exact same observable outcome without intermittent or random failures.\n• **Flakiness (Flaky Test):** A test that intermittently passes and fails on identical application builds without any code changes, usually caused by timing race conditions or shared mutable state.\n• **Quality Gate:** An automated checkpoint in the CI/CD pipeline that blocks flawed builds from progressing to staging or production.",
      "codeSnippet": "function lengthOfLongestSubstring(s: string): number {\n  const map = new Map<string, number>();\n  let maxLen = 0, left = 0;\n  for (let right = 0; right < s.length; right++) {\n    const char = s[right];\n    if (map.has(char) && map.get(char)! >= left) {\n      left = map.get(char)! + 1;\n    }\n    map.set(char, right);\n    maxLen = Math.max(maxLen, right - left + 1);\n  }\n  return maxLen;\n}",
      "bestPractice": "Store the last seen index in a Map to jump the left pointer forward in O(1) rather than sliding one step at a time.",
      "pitfallToAvoid": "Using nested loops to check all substrings, which degrades to O(N^3) time complexity."
